instructions:
CREATE a new presentational React component `ActionBarStats` and its test file. It renders a horizontal strip of five labeled stat chips for a player's remaining resources. It is PURE: no imports from @re/game-core, no store access — only primitive props. It will be mounted inside the ActionBar by another engineer (not you); you only produce these two files.

## FILES TO EMIT (exactly these two paths)

1. `client/src/ui/ActionBarStats.tsx`
2. `client/src/ui/ActionBarStats.test.tsx`

## Component — ActionBarStats.tsx

```tsx
import type { JSX } from 'react';

export interface ActionBarStatsProps {
  /** Current health. */
  readonly hp: number;
  /** Maximum health. */
  readonly maxHp: number;
  /** Gold remaining this turn. */
  readonly gold: number;
  /** Ammo remaining this turn. */
  readonly ammo: number;
  /** Actions remaining this turn. */
  readonly actions: number;
  /** Buys remaining this turn. */
  readonly buys: number;
}
```

Render:

```tsx
export function ActionBarStats({ hp, maxHp, gold, ammo, actions, buys }: ActionBarStatsProps): JSX.Element {
  return (
    <div className="action-stats" data-action-stats>
      <span className="action-stat action-stat-hp" data-stat="hp">HP <strong>{hp}/{maxHp}</strong></span>
      <span className="action-stat action-stat-gold" data-stat="gold">Gold <strong>{gold}</strong></span>
      <span className="action-stat action-stat-ammo" data-stat="ammo">Ammo <strong>{ammo}</strong></span>
      <span className="action-stat action-stat-actions" data-stat="actions">Actions <strong>{actions}</strong></span>
      <span className="action-stat action-stat-buys" data-stat="buys">Buys <strong>{buys}</strong></span>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Rules: named export only, no default export, no classes, no mutation, no extra features. Exactly the JSX above (you may keep it verbatim). The hp chip shows `{hp}/{maxHp}` (e.g. `90/120`); the others show the single number.

## Tests — ActionBarStats.test.tsx

Use the same test style as the existing client UI tests: `import { renderToStaticMarkup } from 'react-dom/server';` and vitest (`describe`, `it`, `expect`). Import the component from `./ActionBarStats`.

Write these cases:

1. it('renders all five stat chips'):
   render `<ActionBarStats hp={90} maxHp={120} gold={40} ammo={30} actions={1} buys={2} />`
   - html contains `data-action-stats`
   - html contains `data-stat="hp"`, `data-stat="gold"`, `data-stat="ammo"`, `data-stat="actions"`, `data-stat="buys"`

2. it('shows hp as current/max'):
   same render — html contains `90/120`

3. it('shows the gold, ammo, actions and buys values'):
   same render — html contains `40`, `30`; and contains `Actions` and `Buys` labels. (Use a distinct-values render to avoid ambiguity: `hp={50} maxHp={80} gold={77} ammo={88} actions={3} buys={4}` and assert html contains `77`, `88`, `50/80`.)

4. it('renders zeros without crashing'):
   `<ActionBarStats hp={0} maxHp={80} gold={0} ammo={0} actions={0} buys={0} />` — html contains `0/80` and contains `data-stat="gold"`.

Serializer facts: React renders `data-stat="hp"` verbatim; text interpolations render inline inside `<strong>` tags, so `90/120` appears as `90<!-- -->/<!-- -->120` in static markup ONLY when built from multiple expressions — to keep tests robust, assert on `90` and `/` and `120` separately OR build the string as a single expression `{hp + '/' + maxHp}` inside the JSX. PREFER the single-expression form `{hp}/{maxHp}` is two expressions — instead write `<strong>{hp}/{maxHp}</strong>` and in tests assert `html).toContain('90')` and `toContain('120')` separately, plus one test asserting the chip element `data-stat="hp"` exists. DO NOT assert on exact `90/120` substring (the comment-node serialization makes it brittle). Adjust test 2 accordingly: assert html contains `90`, `120`, and `data-stat="hp"`.

## VALIDATION (must pass)

- in `client/`: `npx tsc --noEmit`
- in `client/`: `npx vitest run ActionBarStats`
